{"name":"Apple II Magenta","hex":"#dc41f1","fetched":"2026-11-01","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, firm, round fruit produced by a tree of the genus Malus.","The fruit of the tree Malus domestica, chiefly with a green, red, or yellow skin, cultivated in temperate climates for cidermaking, cooking, and eating. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (something) appear like an apple (noun sense 1.1).","To become like an apple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple"},{"term":"magenta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color which is close to the equal mixture of red and blue which is an additive secondary color but a subtractive primary color evoked by the combination of red and light blue."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the color of fuchsia, fuchsine, light purple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magenta"}],"entries":[{"title":"Apple II","blocks":[{"text":"The Apple II is a series of microcomputers manufactured by Apple Computer, Inc. from 1977 to 1993. The original model, designed by Steve Wozniak, first went on sale on June 10, 1977. The series included the Apple II Plus, Apple IIe, Apple IIc, Apple IIc Plus, and Apple IIGS. The IIe was the series' best-selling model. The Apple II name was stylized as the apple ][ on early models and as the Apple // beginning with the IIe in 1983."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg/330px-Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_II","wikidata":"Q201652"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"},{"title":"Magenta","blocks":[{"text":"Magenta is a purplish-pink color. On color wheels of the RGB and CMYK color models, it is located directly midway between blue and red. It is one of the four colors of subtractive ink used in color printing by most color printers, also known as CMYK along with yellow, cyan, and black to make all the other colors and hues. The tone of magenta used in printing, printer's magenta, is redder than the magenta of the RGB (additive) model, the former being closer to rose."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Pink_swanboat_hosting_a_real_bird_on_its_head%2C_on_Shinobazu_Pond%2C_Ueno_Park%2C_Tokyo.jpg/330px-Pink_swanboat_hosting_a_real_bird_on_its_head%2C_on_Shinobazu_Pond%2C_Ueno_Park%2C_Tokyo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magenta","wikidata":"Q3276756"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg/500px-Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg/1280px-Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg/500px-Apple_II-IMG_7064.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"ht